## Why This Is Asked
Tests system design for real-time fraud scoring at scale with privacy constraints, multi-tenant data sharing, and safe rollbacks.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming feature store and time-window features
- Hybrid inference paths: server-side and on-device
- Federated learning with differential privacy
- Drift detection and canary-based rollout strategies
- Model calibration and per-transaction explainability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo scoring sketch
function score(tx, globalModel, localModel){
  const feats = extract(tx)
  const s1 = globalModel.predict(feats)
  const s2 = localModel.predict(feats)
  return calibrate((0.6*s1 + 0.4*s2));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle label latency for online updates?
- What metrics trigger rollback and how would you test rollbacks?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests on-device ML design under strict latency, privacy, and fairness constraints; requires end-to-end thinking from data flow to deployment.

## Key Concepts
- Edge ML and latency budgets
- Federated learning with DP
- Model compression (quantization/pruning)
- Fairness-aware ranking
- Rollback and drift monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
// Latency check scaffold (pseudo)
function measureLatency(model, input){
  const t0 = performance.now();
  model.infer(input);
  const t1 = performance.now();
  return t1 - t0;
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you audit fairness across departments while maintaining DP guarantees?
- What metrics and rollback triggers would you define for drift detection?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess ability to design scalable, tenant-aware ML pipelines with calibration, streaming features, drift handling, and safe rollout strategies across enterprise-scale tenants.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant feature stores and leakage prevention
- Shared base model + per-tenant calibrator
- Real-time features from streaming usage and billing
- Online retraining with drift detectors
- Offline + online evaluation and staged rollout

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo calibration hook
function calibrate(probs, tenantId, store){
  const params = store.getParams(tenantId)
  // adjust by temp scaler
  return Math.min(0.999, Math.max(0.001, probs * params.scale + params.bias))
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate per-tenant calibration offline and online?
- How would you guard against cross-tenant data leakage in CV and feature drift?

## Why This Is Asked
In production, data distributions shift during events; online adaptation with tight latency is essential for edge-scale detectors. This tests practical drift handling and monitoring under real constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Online learning with bounded memory
- Concept drift detection (ADWIN, EDDM)
- Catastrophic forgetting mitigation (replay buffers, ensembles)
- Online calibration (isotonic regression, Platt scaling)
- Streaming infra and latency targets

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: online drift detector usage
let detector = new ADWIN(0.002);
let model = new OnlineModel();
function onInstance(x, y){
  let pred = model.predict(x);
  detector.update(Math.abs(pred - y));
  if (detector.driftDetected()){
     model.updateWithReplayBuffer();
  }
  model.partialFit(x, y);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you compare drift detectors under varying stream rates?
- What metrics would you surface in production dashboards to detect degradation quickly?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests designing a practical, on-device ML flow that balances accuracy, size, privacy, and deployment realities.

## Key Concepts
- On-device constraints: memory, CPU, quantization
- Feature engineering: cyclical time, geospatial bucketing, hashed categories
- Model choice: compact linear models with proper regularization
- Privacy-aware validation: leave-one-user-out or per-session splits
- Evaluation: AUROC, calibration, appropriate thresholds

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tiny logistic regression dot-product (illustrative)
function predict(w, x) {
  let s = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < w.length; i++) s += w[i] * x[i];
  return 1 / (1 + Math.exp(-s));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle missing values on-device?
- How would you validate robustness to distribution shifts after deployment?
